= On a Heuristic Viewpoint: Light Quanta

#text(size: 8.5pt, fill: ink-soft, tracking: 0.12em)[
  ANNALEN DER PHYSIK · SENT 17 MARCH 1905
]
#v(0.5em)

Maxwell's theory @maxwell1865 spreads the energy of light continuously over an
ever-growing volume; matter we count, atom by atom. Yet Planck's law of the
normal spectrum was won only by parcelling energy into discrete elements
@planck1901. I take the parcels seriously: monochromatic radiation behaves, in
its production and transformation, as if it consisted of independent quanta,
each of frequency $nu$ carrying

$ E = h nu $

== The photoelectric effect

Herr Lenard finds @lenard1902 that light upon a clean metal frees electrons at
once, and that the swiftest electron's energy follows the _colour_ of the
light, never its brightness. Each electron is paid by one quantum and keeps
what remains after the work $phi$ owed to the metal:

$ E_"max" = h nu - phi $

Below $nu_0 = phi / h$ nothing escapes, however bright the lamp.

#v(0.5em)
#text(size: 8.5pt, style: "italic", fill: ink-soft)[
  NB. --- Ask whether Lenard's threshold data admit a sharper test; and soften
  the word "heuristic" nowhere. The proofs must go out regardless.
]
